<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Entitlements for the bundled CPython interpreter
         (Contents/Resources/python/bin/python3.13).

         Deliberately empty. Entitlements are per-binary and are NOT inherited
         from the host app, so the interpreter needs its own file — but it
         needs nothing in it: the app's own entitlements already cover the
         network, and every dylib and .so the interpreter loads is re-signed
         with the same Team ID, so library validation passes unaided.

         If a Developer ID build ever fails at launch with a dyld
         "code signature ... not valid for use in process" error, the single
         exception to add is:

             <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
             <true/>

         Add it only in response to an actual failure. It is the weakest
         hardening exception available and shipping it speculatively would
         let any same-path unsigned library load into the interpreter. -->
</dict>
</plist>
